## Explanation
In C programming, `#include` is a **preprocessor directive**. This means it's an instruction given to the C preprocessor, which is a program that processes your source code before the actual compilation step. When the preprocessor encounters an `#include` directive, it essentially replaces that line with the entire content of the specified file.

The primary use of `#include` is to incorporate "header files" into your program. Header files typically contain declarations for functions, macros, and variables that are part of a library or another module. By including them, you make these declarations available to your compiler, allowing it to understand how to use functions like `printf` without needing to see their full implementation in your current file. This helps organize code and promotes reusability.

## Visual Model
```mermaid
graph TD
    A[Your C Source File.c] --> B{C Preprocessor};
    B --> C[Finds #include <header.h>];
    C --> D[Reads Content of header.h];
    D --> B;
    B --> E[Expanded Source Code];
    E --> F[C Compiler];
    F --> G[Object File];
```

## Example from Class
