## Explanation

Swapping two variables means exchanging their values. For instance, if variable `a` holds 10 and `b` holds 20, after swapping, `a` will hold 20 and `b` will hold 10. While the provided reference material doesn't have a dedicated section on "swapping variables," we can achieve this using fundamental C concepts like variable declaration and assignment operators, which are covered in "01_variables_and_types.md" and "04_operators.md" respectively.

The most common and straightforward method involves using a third, temporary variable. This temporary variable acts as a placeholder to safely store one of the original values while the exchange takes place. The process typically involves three steps: first, store the value of the first variable into the temporary variable; second, assign the value of the second variable to the first variable; and finally, assign the value from the temporary variable to the second variable. This ensures no data is lost during the exchange.

## Visual Model
```mermaid
graph TD
    A[Start] --> B{Declare temp variable};
    B --> C{temp = var1};
    C --> D{var1 = var2};
    D --> E{var2 = temp};
    E --> F[End];

    subgraph Value Flow
        V1[var1's initial value] --> T[temp];
        V2[var2's initial value] --> V1_new[var1's new value];
        T --> V2_new[var2's new value];
    end
```

## Example from Class

Since the provided reference material does not contain a direct example of swapping variables, here is a new C code example demonstrating the common method using a temporary variable. This example utilizes variable declaration and initialization as described in "01_variables_and_types.md" and the assignment operator (`=`) from "04_operators.md".

```c
#include <stdio.h> // Needed for printf

int main() {
    // Declare and initialize two integer variables
    // as discussed in "01_variables_and_types.md"
    int num1 = 10;
    int num2 = 20;

    printf("Before swap:\n");
    printf("num1 = %d, num2 = %d\n", num1, num2); // Using %d for integers

    // --- Swapping logic using a temporary variable ---
    // 1. Declare a temporary variable to hold one value
    int temp; 

    // 2. Store the value of num1 in temp
    // This uses the assignment operator '=' from "04_operators.md"
    temp = num1; 

    // 3. Assign the value of num2 to num1
    num1 = num2;

    // 4. Assign the value of temp (which was num1's original value) to num2
    num2 = temp;

    printf("\nAfter swap:\n");
    printf("num1 = %d, num2 = %d\n", num1, num2);

    return 0;
}
```

