Here are the solutions in the most common programming languages.

### The Logic (Algorithm)
Regardless of the language, the standard manual approach is:
1.  Assume the **first element** is the maximum.
2.  Loop through the rest of the array.
3.  If the current number is greater than your assumed maximum, update the maximum.

---

### Python
Python has a built-in function, which is the preferred method.
```python
arr = [3, 1, 40, 15, 9]

# Method 1: Built-in (Recommended)
print(max(arr)) 

# Method 2: Manual Loop
maximum = arr[0]
for num in arr:
    if num > maximum:
        maximum = num
print(maximum)
```

### JavaScript
```javascript
const arr = [3, 1, 40, 15, 9];

// Method 1: Math.max with Spread Operator
console.log(Math.max(...arr));

// Method 2: Manual Loop
let max = arr[0];
for (let i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {
    if (arr[i] > max) {
        max = arr[i];
    }
}
console.log(max);
```

### Java
```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {3, 1, 40, 15, 9};

        // Initialize max with the first element
        int max = arr[0];

        // Loop starting from the second element
        for (int i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {
            if (arr[i] > max) {
                max = arr[i];
            }
        }
        System.out.println(max);
    }
}
```

### C++
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm> // for max_element

int main() {
    std::vector<int> arr = {3, 1, 40, 15, 9};

    // Method 1: STL (Standard Template Library)
    int max_val = *std::max_element(arr.begin(), arr.end());
    std::cout << max_val << std::endl;

    // Method 2: Manual Loop
    int max = arr[0];
    for (int i = 1; i < arr.size(); i++) {
        if (arr[i] > max) {
            max = arr[i];
        }
    }

    return 0;
}
```

### Time Complexity
*   **Time:** $O(n)$ — You have to check every number in the array once.
*   **Space:** $O(1)$ — You only need one variable to store the current maximum.

