If you’re using C or C++, here’s a compact guide.

Core ideas (C/C++)
- Declaration: type* p;  (pointer to type)
- Address-of operator: &x gives the address of x
- Dereference operator: *p accesses the object pointed to by p
- Null pointer: NULL (C) or nullptr (C++). Never dereference a null pointer.

Basics
- int x = 42;
- int* p = &x;     // p holds the address of x
- *p = 7;          // writes to x via p
- if (p) { /* safe to use */ }

Arrays and pointer arithmetic
- int a[3] = {10, 20, 30};
- int* q = a;           // same as &a[0]
- q[1] == *(q + 1);     // -> 20
- q++;                  // now points to a[1]

Passing to functions
- void inc(int* v) { (*v)++; }
- int x = 5; inc(&x);   // x becomes 6

Dynamic memory
- C: int* p = malloc(n * sizeof *p); /* use p[0..n-1] */ free(p);
- C++: int* p = new int(5); delete p;
- C++ arrays: int* a = new int[n]; delete[] a;
- Prefer C++ RAII: std::vector<int> v(n); or smart pointers (std::unique_ptr).

Struct pointers and ->
- struct Point { int x, y; };
- struct Point pt = {1,2};
- struct Point* pp = &pt;
- pp->x = 3;  // same as (*pp).x = 3;

Const correctness
- const int* p: pointer to const int (you can’t modify the int via p)
- int* const p: const pointer to int (p can’t point elsewhere)
- const int* const p: neither can change

Common pitfalls
- Uninitialized pointers: always initialize (to nullptr/NULL or a valid address)
- Dangling pointers: don’t use pointers after the target goes out of scope or after free/delete
- Double free/delete: free/delete exactly once
- Out-of-bounds pointer arithmetic: only move within the same allocated object/array
- Mixing new/delete with malloc/free is undefined behavior

