Short answer: In C-like switch statements (C/C++, Java, JavaScript, C#), put break at the end of each case block to stop execution from “falling through” to the next case.

Example (C/Java/JavaScript/C# style):
switch (n) {
  case 1:
    doSomething();
    break;               // exit the switch here
  case 2:
  case 3:
    doFor2and3();        // combined cases
    break;
  default:
    handleDefault();
    break;               // optional at the end
}

Without break (fall-through):
switch (n) {
  case 1:
    doA();
    // no break
  case 2:
    doB();   // runs for n == 1 or 2
}

Notes by language:
- C, C++, Java, JavaScript, PHP: Use break to prevent fall-through. You can also end a case with return, throw, or goto case (where supported).
- C#: Similar, but the compiler requires each case to end with a jump (break/return/throw/goto).
- Go: Switch breaks automatically; use the keyword fallthrough to continue into the next case.
- Swift: No implicit fall-through; break is optional; use fallthrough to continue.
- Kotlin (when) and Rust (match): No fall-through; no break needed.
- Python (match): No fall-through; break doesn’t apply (break only affects loops).

Inside loops:
- break inside a switch only exits the switch, not an enclosing loop. To break the loop too:
  - Use a labeled break where supported (e.g., Java):
    outer: for (...) {
      switch (x) {
        case 1: break outer; // breaks the loop
      }
    }
  - Or return from a function if appropriate.

Tips:
- Group multiple case labels to avoid duplication.
- Prefer return/throw when a case can finish the function; then you don’t need break.
- If you intentionally omit break to fall through (in languages that allow it), comment it clearly to avoid bugs.

